```

### How do you fix a hot partition?

When the key must stay for ordering but one key is naturally huge, use a composite key to spread the load.

```java
package com.bank.partitioner;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerRecord;

public class CompositeKeyStrategy {
    private static final int BUCKETS = 4;

    public static String compositeKey(String accountId, String transactionId) {
        int bucket = Math.abs(transactionId.hashCode()) % BUCKETS;
        return accountId + "-" + bucket;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // High-volume corporate account spread across 4 partitions
        String corpAccount = "CORP0001";
        for (int i = 1; i <= 8; i++) {
            String txnId = "TXN" + i;
            String key = compositeKey(corpAccount, txnId);
            System.out.println("  " + txnId + " -> key: " + key);
        }
        System.out.println("  One account spread across up to " + BUCKETS + " partitions");
    }
}
```

```text
  TXN1 -> key: CORP0001-1
  TXN2 -> key: CORP0001-2
  TXN3 -> key: CORP0001-3
  TXN4 -> key: CORP0001-0
  TXN5 -> key: CORP0001-1
  TXN6 -> key: CORP0001-2
  TXN7 -> key: CORP0001-3
  TXN8 -> key: CORP0001-0
  One account spread across up to 4 partitions
```

### How do you write a custom Partitioner?

```java
package com.bank.partitioner;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.Partitioner;
import org.apache.kafka.common.Cluster;
import org.apache.kafka.common.utils.Utils;
import java.util.Map;

public class TierPartitioner implements Partitioner {
    private static final int PREMIUM_PARTITIONS = 2;

    @Override
    public int partition(String topic, Object key, byte[] keyBytes,
                         Object value, byte[] valueBytes, Cluster cluster) {
        int numPartitions = cluster.partitionCountForTopic(topic);
        if (key != null && key.toString().startsWith("PREMIUM-")) {
            // Route premium accounts to first 2 partitions
            return Math.abs(Utils.murmur2(keyBytes)) % PREMIUM_PARTITIONS;
        }
        // Route regular accounts to remaining partitions
        return PREMIUM_PARTITIONS +
            (Math.abs(Utils.murmur2(keyBytes)) % (numPartitions - PREMIUM_PARTITIONS));
    }

    @Override public void close() { }
    @Override public void configure(Map<String, ?> configs) { }
}
```

```text
// Configure in producer:
// props.put(ProducerConfig.PARTITIONER_CLASS_CONFIG, TierPartitioner.class.getName());
// PREMIUM-SAV2001 -> partition 0 or 1 (dedicated capacity)
// SAV2001         -> partition 2..11  (regular pool)
```

### What are the rules and constraints?

- Ordering is only within a partition, never across partitions. Global ordering requires a single partition, which destroys parallelism.
- Changing the partition count rehashes keys, so records for a key can move to a different partition.
- A null key means round-robin (or sticky) partitioning with no ordering guarantee.
- The partition count is a capacity decision made early because increasing it later disrupts key mapping.

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer recognizes that the key is a design decision that simultaneously controls correctness through ordering and performance through distribution. Key choice should be documented in the topic contract. When a single dominant key cannot be split, the senior approach is a composite key or a custom partitioner, with explicit acknowledgment of the ordering trade-off. Monitoring per-partition lag, not just aggregate lag, is how you detect a hot partition before it becomes an outage.
